            <ArticleTitle>An evaluation of the Veterans Affairs traumatic brain injury screening process among Operation Enduring Freedom and/or Operation Iraqi Freedom veterans.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>210-20; quiz 220</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2012.12.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(12)01773-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the early results of the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) screening program for traumatic brain injury (TBI) and to identify patient and facility characteristics associated with receiving a TBI screen and results of the screening.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">National retrospective cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">VA Medical facilities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 170,681 Operation Enduring Freedom and/or Operation Iraqi Freedom (OEF/OIF) Veterans who sought care at VA medical facilities from April 2007 to September 30, 2008.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were abstracted from VA administrative and operational databases, including patient demographics, facility characteristics, and outcomes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The main outcomes were receipt of and results of the TBI screen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The majority of veterans eligible received the TBI screen (91.6%). Screening rates varied by patient and facility characteristics. In all, 25% of screened veterans had probable TBI exposure, in which the majority of the exposures were blasts (85.0%). The rate of a positive TBI screen was 20.5% for the screened cohort. Male gender, service in the army, multiple deployments, and mental health diagnoses in the previous year were associated with a positive screen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">TBI screening rates are high in VA; concomitant mental health diagnoses were highly prevalent in individuals with positive TBI screens. These data indicate that there will be a significant need for long-term health care services for veterans with TBI symptomatology.</AbstractText>
